found-at-dawn.intro:
[who:young-man] doesn't wake.

He's lying the way he lay down, {if [q:prologue-young-man-under-your-coat] > 0}under your coat, pulled up to his chin{else}blanket to his chin{/if}. His face is grey and wet. The cough took him in the night, quietly, while the fire was out and everyone slept.{if [q:prologue-young-man-fed] > 0} The heel of the bread you gave him is still by his hand.{/if}

[who:regard-rope-mother] crouches and feels inside his shirt for a seal. Nothing. "No claim," she says. "He was going to sign today."{if [q:prologue-young-man-surgeon] > 0} She looks at you. "Your surgeon wouldn't have him. Nobody would. Not without a seal."{/if}{if [q:prologue-young-man-cared] >= 2} She shakes her head. "You did more for him than anyone. It was never going to be enough, out here."{/if}{if [q:prologue-young-man-cared] == 0} "All summer on the ground," she says, to nobody. "And nobody had anything for him."{/if} She looks up at the sky. "We've till noon, maybe. Then he gets up."

the-camp-afterwards.intro:
{if [q:prologue-sold-the-young-man] > 0}Before the sun is fully up, the whole camp knows what you sold, and for how much. Some of them look at you like a thief. More of them look at you like someone with six coin.{/if}{if [q:prologue-binder-released-the-young-man] > 0}When you come out, two hand-carts stand at the chapel door, as on most mornings: an old woman the cold took in the night, and a boy from the dead-yard with his chest stove in. [who:regard-binder] kneels by the first. She is slower at it than she was with [who:young-man].{/if}{if [q:prologue-burned-the-young-man] > 0}The haulers don't talk about it. They don't need to. When the pot goes round, it comes to you first.{/if}{if [q:prologue-bound-the-young-man] > 0}Nobody comes near your blanket. [who:young-man] stands at the edge of the haulers' fire, where you left him, waiting for you.{/if}{if [q:prologue-left-the-young-man] > 0}There's a new mound in the ditch by the road. The camp walks round it. Nobody walks round you, exactly. But nobody sits by you, either.{/if}{if [q:prologue-young-man-cared] >= 3}

At the fire, somebody says you did more for him these last days than his own people would have. Nobody argues.{/if}{if [q:prologue-young-man-cared] == 0}

At the fire, somebody says he'd been coughing since the summer, and nobody did a thing. Nobody looks at anybody when he says it.{/if}

Up at the land gate, the wardens are letting the morning's arrivals through, one mark at a time.

the-chapel-at-the-gate.intro:
A chapel built into the wall beside the gate, so that the first thing in is a prayer and the last thing out is a seal. Incense, and under it, cold stone.

{if [q:met-binder] == 0}A hand-cart stands at the door, with an old man on it, dead of the cold, and a family round him with nothing in their hands. Two empty carts lean on the wall beside it: the morning's. A young woman in grey kneels by the old man, lays a seal on his brow, says the words, and drives a nail through it. The dead man opens his eyes, and in the same breath she draws out the nail and the seal, and he lies still for good.

She gets up slowly and goes back to her table, and holds her hands over the fire pan there, and they stay blue. Her hair is grey too, though she can't be thirty.{else}[who:regard-binder] sits at her table with the stamp and the box of seals, holding her hands over the fire pan.{if [q:chill-binder] >= 1} She keeps her hands closer to the coals than she did.{/if}{if [q:prologue-bed] >= 1} Behind her, a low door in the wall: her room.{/if}{/if}{if [q:crisis-claim-talk] > 0}

Since the news, the line for church claims goes out of the door and down past the hand-carts. Everyone in it has heard the same talk: bread by the claim.{/if}

the-fleet-is-lost.intro:
The wardens take the rider up the road and in through the gate, and the crowd stands looking after him, as if the gate might say something else.

By the next bell the baker has wiped his board. People who had a coin put by are spending it. People who hadn't stand and watch them. At the chapel the line for church claims goes out past the hand-carts, and at every fire it's the same talk, passed along like a bucket: the council will give out bread by the claim. The companies will feed their own, and the church its own.

Nobody says what happens to the rest. Nobody needs to.{if [q:claim] == 0} Your hand goes to your neck, where a seal would hang.{/if}
